+ "render:watercolor:prompt": "PRIORITY RULES:\n1) No text or symbols anywhere: no letters, numbers, signage, logos or inscriptions, no writing on labels, bottles, books, signs, slates or playing cards, and no artist's signature or initials in a corner.\n2) One scene painted to every edge of the frame: no panels, collage, border, frame, margin, visible paper or blank backdrop, and never a photographed painting or mockup.\n3) Nothing from outside the moment's era.\n4) Natural anatomy: no extra or malformed limbs, hands or faces.\n5) sexually explicit content and lingering gore are never shown; serious depictions of battles, blood and death and non-sexual nudity may be, treated seriously.\n6) No likeness of any real actor, performer or public figure.\n7) Show the SELECTED CHARACTERS with their canon. Anyone else appears only as an unnamed figure the scene plan describes.\nIf instructions conflict, follow these rules, then the work brief, then canon, then the art style, then the scene plan. The text decides who is in the scene; canon decides how they look; the scene plan decides this moment's place, time, light, clothing and action. Owner look constraints, when given, are binding.\n\nSCENE: one moment of alice-in-wonderland, Pig and Pepper ¶50: \"The Cat only grinned when it saw Alice. It looked good-natured, she thought: still it had very long claws and a great many teeth, so she felt that it ought to be treated with respect.\"\n\nWORK BRIEF (alice-in-wonderland; what may be shown; it outranks the style, the look and the plan):\nTone: Whimsical and dreamlike, with a gently unsettling bewilderment that turns to sharp absurdity at court.\nDepiction: Alice's changing size reads clearly against doors, furniture, trees and other figures.\nDepiction: Talking animals are drawn as animals, with human posture and dress where the text gives it.\nDepiction: Playing-card courtiers, gardeners and soldiers are flat, oblong cards with heads, arms and legs.\nDepiction: Threats and executions stay threats or slapstick; no gore or real injury.\nDepiction: Take no likeness, costume or prop from film or animated adaptations.\nEra: Victorian England and its dream Wonderland\nPeople: Alice, a young Victorian English girl, among talking animals, card courtiers and odd Wonderland folk.\nDress: A Victorian child's dress and pinafore for Alice; period robes, waistcoats and hats for others.\nBuildings and objects: Riverbank, rabbit-hole, long hall, little door, cottages, mushroom wood, croquet-ground and courtroom.\nTechnology: Pocket watches, oil lamps, slates and pencils; no machines or modern devices.\n\nSELECTED CHARACTERS: Alice; Cheshire Cat\n\nCANON (fixed identity of the figures, places and things present; the scene plan decides pose, expression and action):\n- Alice: a girl of about seven with long light-brown hair, in a blue dress and white pinafore.\n- Cheshire Cat: a large striped cat with a very wide grin, long claws and many teeth.\n- the Duchess's wood: tall old trees with low boughs and dappled light.\n- Only Alice, Cheshire Cat, the Duchess's wood have this canon look; other selected people get clearly different faces, hair and clothing colours.\n\nSCENE PLAN (this moment's place, time and action; obey the rules, brief, canon and style):\nthe Cheshire Cat grinning on a bough; grins down at Alice; a wood of old trees; Alice looking up respectfully; curious; afternoon; Alice small below, the Cat above\n\nART STYLE: Ink & Watercolor\nRendering: Pen-and-ink drawing with transparent watercolor washes laid over the whole picture; a finished watercolor painting of the work's world, not a sketch.\nNegatives: Washes, pigment and scenery must reach every edge. No white or cream paper margins, unpainted corners, paper edges, sketchbook, tape, border or vignette. Do not present the scene on a sheet of paper or against a blank backdrop. Avoid photorealism, digital airbrush smoothness and cartoon caricature.\nPalette: Transparent washes of ochre, sienna, indigo and sage covering the whole picture, sky and corners included.\nLighting: Clear natural light; shadows as cool layered washes.\nMaterials and texture: Confident ink linework, wet-in-wet blooms and granulating pigment within the depicted world.\nComposition: A complete scene: setting, sky and ground painted out to every edge and corner, with detail concentrated on key subjects.\n\nNARRATIVE CONTINUITY (cues only; compose the current moment):\n- After: “Cheshire Puss,” she began, rather timidly, as she did not at all know whether it would like the name.\n\nVariation note: this is image 2 of this moment. Keep the same canonical scene, but vary composition and camera feel.\n\nSCENE PRESENTATION:\n- Depict the moment directly, as if the viewer is present inside the scene.\n- Extend scenery, sky, darkness, architecture or atmosphere all the way to the edges and corners.\nAspect ratio: 16:9 (WIDESCREEN landscape).",
