ole\":\"user\",\"content\":\"DEPICTION RULES\\n- Follow the narrated moment, including clues and disguises; avoid sensational gore.\\n- Show every person with dignity; avoid racial and ethnic caricatures.\\n- Do not borrow faces, costumes or props from screen adaptations.\\nSTORY SECTION: Holmes's deductions on Watson's marriage\\n\\nMOMENT (A Scandal in Bohemia, Part I ¶1): One night I was returning from a journey to a patient, when my way led me through Baker Street. As I passed the well-remembered door, I was seized with a keen desire to see Holmes again.\\nPREVIOUS: (section starts here)\\nNEXT: His rooms were brilliantly lit, and, even as I looked up, I saw his tall, spare figure pass twice in a dark silhouette against the blind.\\n\\nSECTION CAST (id: name, hasForm):\\n- sherlock_holmes: Sherlock Holmes (hasForm: true)\\n- dr_watson: Dr. Watson (hasForm: true)\\n- baker_street: 221B Baker Street (hasForm: true)\\n\\nSELECTED CANON (complete assigned stages; fixed identity, not source evidence):\\n- dr_watson (stage: main, kind: character, hasForm: true): Dr. Watson: sturdy, moustached, in a brown tweed suit.\\n- baker_street (stage: main, kind: location, hasForm: true): 221B Baker Street: a first-floor sitting-room with a bow window and a coal fire.\\n\\nPLAN\\npresent: dr_watson, baker_street\\nprimarySubject: Watson pausing on the pavement\\naction: looks up at the lit window\\nsetting: Baker Street at night in the rain\\nsecondaryElements: a tall silhouette on the blind above\\nmood: nostalgic\\ntimeOfDay: night, by gaslight\\ncomposition: low view up to the bow window\"}],\"response_format\":{\"type\":\"json_schema\",\"json_schema\":{\"name\":\"plan_review\",\"strict\":true,\"schema\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"problems\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"kind\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"unselected_figure\",\"outside_instant\",\"not_one_instant\",\"look_conflict\",\"canon_conflict\",\"missing_canon\"]},\"entryId\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The cast id the problem concerns; empty when it has none.\"},\"planQuote\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"A short exact phrase from a look field for look_conflict; otherwise from a scene field or a selected id.\"},\"reason\":{\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"kind\",\"entryId\",\"planQuote\",\"reason\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},\"required\":[\"problems\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},\"provider\":{\"require_parameters\":true,\"order\":[\"anthropic\"],\"allow_fallbacks\":false},\"reasoning\":{\"effort\":\"low\"},\"max_tokens\":4000,\"usage\":{\"include\":true}}",
- "render:watercolor:prompt": "PRIORITY RULES:\n1) No text or symbols anywhere: no letters, numbers, signage, logos or inscriptions, no writing on letters, newspapers, books, signs or boards, and no artist's signature or initials in a corner.\n2) One scene painted to every edge of the frame: no panels, collage, border, frame, margin, visible paper or blank backdrop, and never a photographed painting or mockup.\n3) Nothing from outside the moment's era.\n4) Natural anatomy: no extra or malformed limbs, hands or faces.\n5) sexually explicit content and lingering gore are never shown; serious depictions of battles, blood and death and non-sexual nudity may be, treated seriously.\n6) No likeness of any real actor, performer or public figure.\n7) Show the SELECTED CHARACTERS with their canon. Anyone else appears only as an unnamed figure the scene plan describes.\nIf instructions conflict, follow these rules, then the work brief, then canon, then the art style, then the scene plan. The text decides who is in the scene; canon decides how they look; the scene plan decides this moment's place, time, light, clothing and action. Owner look constraints, when given, are binding.\n\nSCENE: one moment of holmes-adventures, A Scandal in Bohemia, Part I ¶1: \"One night I was returning from a journey to a patient, when my way led me through Baker Street. As I passed the well-remembered door, I was seized with a keen desire to see Holmes again.\"\n\nWORK BRIEF (holmes-adventures; what may be shown; it outranks the style, the look and the plan):\nTone: Observant, atmospheric detective fiction: lamplit rooms, foggy streets, restrained tension and dry wit.\nDepiction: Follow the narrated moment, including clues and disguises; avoid sensational gore.\nDepiction: Show every person with dignity; avoid racial and ethnic caricatures.\nDepiction: Do not borrow faces, costumes or props from screen adaptations.\nEra: Late Victorian England, 1880s to 1890s\nPeople: English households, professionals, workers, police and international visitors.\nDress: Wool coats, waistcoats, hats, period dresses and work clothes suited to occupation and means.\nBuildings and objects: Brick terraces, rural houses, lodgings, coal fires, desks, newspapers and horse-drawn cabs.\nTechnology: Steam rail, gas and oil lamps, telegrams, print and typewriters; other devices only when supported by the text.\n\nSELECTED CHARACTERS: Dr. Watson\n\nCANON (fixed identity of the figures, places and things present; the scene plan decides pose, expression and action):\n- Dr. Watson: sturdy, moustached, in a brown tweed suit.\n- 221B Baker Street: a first-floor sitting-room with a bow window and a coal fire.\n- Only Dr. Watson, 221B Baker Street have this canon look; other selected people get clearly different faces, hair and clothing colours.\n\nSCENE PLAN (this moment's place, time and action; obey the rules, brief, canon and style):\nPrimary subject: Watson pausing on the pavement\nAction: looks up at the lit window\nSetting: Baker Street at night in the rain\nSecondary elements: a tall silhouette on the blind above\nMood: nostalgic\nTime of day: night, by gaslight\nComposition: low view up to the bow window\n\nART STYLE: Ink & Watercolor\nRendering: Pen-and-ink drawing with transparent watercolor washes laid over the whole picture; a finished watercolor painting of the work's world, not a sketch.\nNegatives: Washes, pigment and scenery must reach every edge. No white or cream paper margins, unpainted corners, paper edges, sketchbook, tape, border or vignette. Do not present the scene on a sheet of paper or against a blank backdrop. Avoid photorealism, digital airbrush smoothness and cartoon caricature.\nPalette: Transparent washes of ochre, sienna, indigo and sage covering the whole picture, sky and corners included.\nLighting: Clear natural light; shadows as cool layered washes.\nMaterials and texture: Confident ink linework, wet-in-wet blooms and granulating pigment within the depicted world.\nComposition: A complete scene: setting, sky and ground painted out to every edge and corner, with detail concentrated on key subjects.\n\nNARRATIVE CONTINUITY (cues only; compose the current moment):\n- After: His rooms were brilliantly lit, and, even as I looked up, I saw his tall, spare figure pass twice in a dark silhouette against the blind.\n\nVariation note: this is image 2 of this moment. Keep the same canonical scene, but vary composition and camera feel.\n\nSCENE PRESENTATION:\n- Depict the moment directly, as if the viewer is present inside the scene.\n- Extend scenery, sky, darkness, architecture or atmosphere all the way to the edges and corners.\nAspect ratio: 16:9 (WIDESCREEN landscape).",
